# kumulant working notes

Kotlin Multiplatform streaming statistics library.

## Pre-PR gate

Run `./gradlew check lintDocs`.

- check runs tests and some light checks.
- lintDocs runs detekt and dokka linting.
- For new/edited files add `--rerun-tasks`, detekt caches per source-set and skips them otherwise.

## Tests

- Test names within backticks, plain ascii, describing the behavior under test.
- Class almost always named after the file under test (`<FileUnderTest>Test`).
- One observable behavior per test; assert behavior, not implementation. Follow arrange → act → assert.
- Mirror existing tests' framework, layout, and assertion style.
- Reuse existing fixtures/helpers; don't add new ones unless asked.
- Use the parameterized form for variants of the same behavior; separate tests for semantically different ones.
- Keep comments minimal and let the test name carry the intent.
- No JVM test over 300ms.

## Bench

`:kumulant-bench` holds the measurement tasks, each driving the StatSpec registry and printing to stdout:

- `analyzeAccuracy`, `analyzeThroughput`, `analyzeConcurrencyDrift`, `analyzeMergeContention` for stats.
- `analyzeBanditAccuracy`, `analyzeBanditThroughput`, `analyzeBanditDrift` for bandits.
- `benchmark` (or `jvmBenchmark` / `linuxX64Benchmark`) runs the JMH suites.
- Cite drift and throughput numbers in KDoc only when measured from these tasks; drop them otherwise.

## Design

- A concurrent stat may drift, but must never throw, tear, or drop an update. Put a new stat in one of
  the four categories in `stream/ConcurrencyResolution.kt` and use its helper; don't invent a fifth.
- Weights: zero is a no-op, negative downdates where the metric has an inverse and throws where it
  doesn't. Guard against corruption only, not surprising numbers.
- Stats stay generic primitives; no domain-named stats (Apdex, Sharpe, MTBF, RSI).

## Style

- Prefer clear naming and types over comments. Comments explain *why* (soundness, perf, lifecycle) and never
  restate the code. Never narrate changes, avoid "now/no longer/previously", and don't cite issues. Keep `TODO`/`FIXME`.
- KDoc on public API only. Use functional notation for math (`succ(i)`, not `succ[i]`); use `[Symbol]` for links.
- Stat, bandit, and package-page KDoc follow `docs/internal/STAT_KDOC_TEMPLATE.md`,
  `BANDIT_KDOC_TEMPLATE.md`, and `STAT_PACKAGE_TEMPLATE.md`. Read the matching one before writing docs.
- Don't edit anything under `.github/` unless asked directly.
- Single-line conventional commits, no scope, no body/trailers:
  `feat: `, `fix: `, `chore: `, `refactor: `, `docs: `, `test: `.
- PR body: follow `.github/pull_request_template.md`, with `Closes #NN` on its own line at the top.
